Component 2: dashboard_server.py — "The Messenger"

This is the bridge between Python and the browser. It runs two servers:
HTTP server on port 8000 — serves dashboard.html when you visit http://localhost:8000 in your browser. Simple file server — just hands the HTML file to the browser.
WebSocket server on port 8765 — keeps an open connection with the browser. When the subscriber calls log_valid_message() or log_rejected_message(), this server broadcasts the event to every connected browser tab.

Component 3: subscriber_dashboard.py — "The Security Guard"

This is the most important file. It combines everything from Week 6's (Project 6) subscriber_defended.py with the dashboard integration. The validation logic is identical:
HMAC check — is the signature valid? Catches message tampering.
Timestamp check — is the message fresh? Catches stale replays.
Sequence check — is this a new message? Catches recent replays.

Component 4: publisher_defended.py — "The Sensor"

This one's easy. It's the same publisher as Project 6. Hasn't changed. It still connects to the mTLS broker with its device certificate, generates simulated sensor readings, signs each message with HMAC, increments the sequence counter, and publishes.
The publisher doesn't know the dashboard exists — and it doesn't need to. It just sends data. The subscriber handles everything else.

Week 7 Main Building Live Data 

Time to break it: 
Phase 1: Eavesdrop -The simulator subscribes to hydroficient/grandmarina/# and intercepts legitimate messages from your publisher. It displays them in the terminal with a dramatic "INTERCEPTED" box showing the topic, pressure, flow rate, and timestamp.

Phase 2: Inject Fake Data- The simulator publishes a message with:
A valid-looking device ID (HYDROLOGIC-Device-001)
A current timestamp
A high sequence number (99999)
Dangerous sensor values (250 PSI pressure — double the normal range)
A fake HMAC signature (FAKE_HMAC_0000...)

Phase 3: Replay - 
The simulator re-sends one of the messages it captured during Phase 1. This is a real captured message — it has a valid HMAC, because it was originally signed by the real publisher. But by the time it's replayed, two things have gone wrong:
The timestamp is stale. Several seconds have passed since the original message was sent. The subscriber's 30-second freshness window may catch this, especially if the eavesdropping phase ran long enough.
The sequence number is old. The subscriber already accepted the original message and recorded its sequence number. Seeing the same number again triggers the replay defense.

Recap
- The attack simulator runs three phases: eavesdrop (passive), inject (fake HMAC), and replay (re-send captured message)
- Phase 2 (injection) is caught by HMAC verification — the attacker can't forge the signature without the shared secret
- Phase 3 (replay) is caught by the sequence counter and/or timestamp check — the subscriber rejects duplicate or stale messages
- Every attack should be blocked — the dashboard will show red alerts and increment the "Attacks Blocked" counter
- No single defense stops everything — the three layers (HMAC, timestamp, sequence) work together, and the dashboard makes that visible

